IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wra-core`
IMPORT `wra-definitions`
IMPORT `wra1-three-valued`
IMPORT `wra1-ch3-general`

-- wra1-ch1-entitlement.l4: Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), Part 1, Chapter 1 (sections 1 to 12), part wra1.
-- LAYER 1, the law objectively: it takes nobody's point of view.
-- Source: inputs/ukpga-2012-5.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script, not the official text.
--
-- Reading an answer (rule of 2026-10-29, assumed and not ruled by a lawyer):
--   FALSE / JUST FALSE = the text says no in its own words (quoted in a FALSE-kind comment), or a supplied regulation says no
--   NOTHING            = not covered (the reason is in a NOTHING-kind comment)
-- Values the regulations supply enter as MAYBE inputs (PLAN.md section 7): NOTHING in gives NOTHING out, except where a
-- comment says the Act's own words answer and the regulation only overrides them when supplied (a deeming power).
-- Powers to make regulations that this module does not read (s 7(3), s 9(2), s 10(2)-(3), s 11(3)-(5)(b), s 12(3)-(4)) have
-- no function; their outputs are the inputs below, and NOTES.md gives each its row.

§ `Section 1 -- Universal credit`

-- src:6 | (1) A benefit known as universal credit is payable in accordance with this Part.
@ref s 1(1) (src:6-6)
`s 1(1) the benefit payable in accordance with this Part` MEANS `universal credit`

-- "an individual who is not a member of a couple (a 'single person')": the label is a definition.
-- src:8 | (a) an individual who is not a member of a couple (a “single person”), or
@ref s 1(2)(a) (src:8-8)
GIVEN `the individual is a member of a couple` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 1(2)(a) the individual is a single person` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an individual who is not a member of a couple (a 'single person')"
    NOT `the individual is a member of a couple`

-- Whose award it may be: (a) a single person, or (b) members of a couple jointly. A single claimant who is in fact a member
-- of a couple needs s 2(2) regulations, which are not among the sources.
-- src:7 | (2) Universal credit may, subject as follows, be awarded to—
-- src:8 | (a) an individual who is not a member of a couple (a “single person”), or
-- src:9 | (b) members of a couple jointly.
@ref s 1(2) (src:7-9)
GIVEN claimants IS A Claimants
      `the single claimant is a member of a couple` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 1(2) universal credit may be awarded to these claimants` MEANS
    CONSIDER claimants
    WHEN `a single claimant` person THEN
        IF `s 1(2)(a) the individual is a single person` `the single claimant is a member of a couple`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a member of a couple may claim as a single person only in circumstances regulations specify (s 2(2)); not among the sources
        ELSE NOTHING
    WHEN `joint claimants` two THEN
        IF `s 39(1) the two people are a couple` two
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may ... be awarded to ... (b) members of a couple jointly" does not say that joint claimants who are not a couple may not
        ELSE NOTHING

-- "An award of universal credit is ... calculated by reference to (a) a standard allowance, (b) an amount for responsibility
-- for children or young persons, (c) an amount for housing, and (d) amounts for other particular needs or circumstances."
DECLARE `s 1(3) element of an award` IS ONE OF
    `s 1(3)(a) standard allowance`
    `s 1(3)(b) responsibility for children or young persons`
    `s 1(3)(c) housing`
    `s 1(3)(d) other particular needs or circumstances`

-- src:10 | (3) An award of universal credit is, subject as follows, calculated by reference to—
-- src:11 | (a) a standard allowance,
-- src:12 | (b) an amount for responsibility for children or young persons,
-- src:13 | (c) an amount for housing, and
-- src:14 | (d) amounts for other particular needs or circumstances.
@ref s 1(3) (src:10-14)
`s 1(3) the elements by reference to which an award is calculated` MEANS
    LIST `s 1(3)(a) standard allowance`
       , `s 1(3)(b) responsibility for children or young persons`
       , `s 1(3)(c) housing`
       , `s 1(3)(d) other particular needs or circumstances`

-- The section of this Part that supplies each element (s 8(2)(a)-(d) names the same four).
@ref s 1(3) (src:10-14)
GIVEN element IS A `s 1(3) element of an award`
GIVETH A STRING
`s 1(3) the section that gives the element` MEANS
    CONSIDER element
    WHEN `s 1(3)(a) standard allowance` THEN "section 9"
    WHEN `s 1(3)(b) responsibility for children or young persons` THEN "section 10"
    WHEN `s 1(3)(c) housing` THEN "section 11"
    WHEN `s 1(3)(d) other particular needs or circumstances` THEN "section 12"

§ `Section 2 -- Claims`

-- Who may claim: the same two classes as s 1(2)(a) and (b), so the answer is s 1(2)'s.
-- src:16 | (1) A claim may be made for universal credit by—
-- src:17 | (a) a single person, or
-- src:18 | (b) members of a couple jointly.
@ref s 2(1) (src:16-18)
GIVEN claimants IS A Claimants
      `the single claimant is a member of a couple` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) a claim may be made by these claimants` MEANS
    `s 1(2) universal credit may be awarded to these claimants` claimants `the single claimant is a member of a couple`

-- s 2(2): the regulations are not among the sources, so the answer they give for this member of a couple is an input.
-- src:19 | (2) Regulations may specify circumstances in which a member of a couple may make a claim as a single person.
@ref s 2(2) (src:19-19)
GIVEN `regulations specify circumstances that cover this member of a couple` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) a member of a couple may make a claim as a single person` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations specify circumstances that cover this member of a couple`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): the regulations the caller supplies do not cover this member of a couple
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may specify circumstances"; they are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `Section 3 -- Entitlement`

-- "A single claimant is entitled to universal credit if the claimant meets (a) the basic conditions, and (b) the financial
-- conditions for a single claimant." Each argument is the answer of s 4(1) and s 5(1) respectively.
-- src:22 | (1) A single claimant is entitled to universal credit if the claimant meets—
-- src:23 | (a) the basic conditions, and
-- src:24 | (b) the financial conditions for a single claimant.
@ref s 3(1) (src:22-24)
GIVEN `the claimant meets the basic conditions` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the claimant meets the financial conditions for a single claimant` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) the single claimant is entitled to universal credit` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is entitled to universal credit if the claimant meets (a) ... and (b) ..." (a failed "if": lawyer's reading (a))
    `s 3(1) both of these are true` `the claimant meets the basic conditions` `the claimant meets the financial conditions for a single claimant`

-- src:25 | (2) Joint claimants are jointly entitled to universal credit if—
-- src:26 | (a) each of them meets the basic conditions, and
-- src:27 | (b) they meet the financial conditions for joint claimants.
@ref s 3(2) (src:25-27)
GIVEN `the first of them meets the basic conditions` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the second of them meets the basic conditions` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `they meet the financial conditions for joint claimants` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(2) joint claimants are jointly entitled to universal credit` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "are jointly entitled to universal credit if (a) each of them meets the basic conditions, and (b) they meet the financial conditions" (a failed "if": lawyer's reading (a))
    `s 3(1) both of these are true`
        (`s 3(1) both of these are true` `the first of them meets the basic conditions` `the second of them meets the basic conditions`)
        `they meet the financial conditions for joint claimants`

§ `Section 4 -- Basic conditions`

-- (a) "is at least 18 years old", and s 4(3): "regulations may specify a different minimum age for prescribed cases".
-- The input is the minimum age the regulations give for this person's case; NOTHING means no such regulation is supplied.
-- src:36 | (3) For the basic condition in subsection (1)(a) regulations may specify a different minimum age for prescribed cases.
@ref s 4(3) (src:36-36)
-- src:29 | (1) For the purposes of section 3, a person meets the basic conditions who—
-- src:30 | (a) is at least 18 years old,
@ref s 4(1)(a) (src:29-30)
GIVEN person IS A Person
      `the different minimum age regulations prescribe for this case under section 4(3)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(a) the person is at least 18 years old` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the different minimum age regulations prescribe for this case under section 4(3)`
    -- the regulation, where supplied, replaces the Act's 18 in the prescribed case
    WHEN JUST minimum THEN JUST (person's `age in years` AT LEAST minimum)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF person's `age in years` AT LEAST 18
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): under 18 the "no" is provisional, since regulations may specify a different minimum age for prescribed cases (s 4(3))
        ELSE NOTHING

-- (b) "has not reached the qualifying age for state pension credit"; s 4(4) points at section 1(6) of the State Pension
-- Credit Act 2002, which is not among the sources, so whether the person has reached it is a fact asked.
-- src:37 | (4) For the basic condition in subsection (1)(b), the qualifying age for state pension credit is that referred to in section 1(6) of the State Pension Credit Act 2002.
@ref s 4(4) (src:37-37)
-- src:31 | (b) has not reached the qualifying age for state pension credit,
@ref s 4(1)(b) (src:31-31)
GIVEN `the person has reached the qualifying age for state pension credit` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(b) the person has not reached the qualifying age for state pension credit` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "has not reached the qualifying age for state pension credit"
    NOT `the person has reached the qualifying age for state pension credit`

-- (c) "is in Great Britain": England, Scotland and Wales, not Northern Ireland and not outside the United Kingdom.
-- src:32 | (c) is in Great Britain,
@ref s 4(1)(c) (src:32-32)
GIVEN place IS A Place
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(c) the place is in Great Britain` MEANS
    CONSIDER place
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(c) is in Great Britain": Northern Ireland is not in Great Britain
    WHEN `Northern Ireland` THEN FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(c) is in Great Britain": a place outside the United Kingdom is not in Great Britain
    WHEN `outside the United Kingdom` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 4(5)(a)-(b): regulations may treat a person as being or not being in Great Britain, and may disregard temporary absence.
-- Each is an input; NOTHING means no such regulation is supplied, and then the person's place decides.
-- src:38 | (5) For the basic condition in subsection (1)(c) regulations may—
-- src:39 | (a) specify circumstances in which a person is to be treated as being or not being in Great Britain;
-- src:40 | (b) specify circumstances in which temporary absence from Great Britain is disregarded;
@ref s 4(5)(a)-(b) (src:38-40)
-- src:32 | (c) is in Great Britain,
@ref s 4(1)(c) (src:32-32)
GIVEN person IS A Person
      `regulations treat the person as being or not being in Great Britain` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the absence is temporary and regulations disregard it` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(c) the person is in Great Britain` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations treat the person as being or not being in Great Britain`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): regulations treat the person as not being in Great Britain (s 4(5))
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF `s 4(1)(c) the place is in Great Britain` (person's `where the person is`)
        THEN TRUE
        ELSE
            CONSIDER `the absence is temporary and regulations disregard it`
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(c) is in Great Britain": the person is not in Great Britain and no temporary absence is disregarded
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- (d) "is not receiving education": what "receiving education" means is left to regulations (s 4(6)(a)), and regulations
-- may treat a person as receiving or not receiving it (s 4(6)(b)); both are inputs.
-- src:42 | (6) For the basic condition in subsection (1)(d) regulations may—
-- src:43 | (a) specify what “receiving education” means;
-- src:44 | (b) specify circumstances in which a person is to be treated as receiving or not receiving education.
@ref s 4(6) (src:42-44)
-- src:33 | (d) is not receiving education, and
@ref s 4(1)(d) (src:33-33)
GIVEN `the person is receiving education within the meaning regulations give` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `regulations treat the person as receiving or not receiving education` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(d) the person is not receiving education` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations treat the person as receiving or not receiving education`
    WHEN JUST x THEN `s 4(1)(d) the opposite of this` (JUST x)
    -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "receiving education" has such meaning as regulations specify (s 4(6)(a)); they are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `s 4(1)(d) the opposite of this` `the person is receiving education within the meaning regulations give`

-- (e) "has accepted a claimant commitment": s 14(5) says when a claimant accepts one; s 4(7) lets regulations treat a person as
-- having accepted or not accepted.
-- src:45 | (7) For the basic condition in subsection (1)(e) regulations may specify circumstances in which a person is to be treated as having accepted or not accepted a claimant commitment.
@ref s 4(7) (src:45-45)
-- src:34 | (e) has accepted a claimant commitment.
@ref s 4(1)(e) (src:34-34)
GIVEN `the person accepts the claimant commitment as section 14(5) requires` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `regulations treat the person as having accepted or not accepted a claimant commitment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1)(e) the person has accepted a claimant commitment` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations treat the person as having accepted or not accepted a claimant commitment`
    WHEN JUST x THEN JUST x
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the person accepts the claimant commitment as section 14(5) requires`

-- "a person meets the basic conditions who" meets all five. s 4(2): "Regulations may provide for exceptions to the requirement
-- to meet any of the basic conditions" (and, for joint claimants, for one or both). That makes a "no" provisional.
-- src:35 | (2) Regulations may provide for exceptions to the requirement to meet any of the basic conditions (and, for joint claimants, may provide for an exception for one or both).
@ref s 4(2) (src:35-35)
-- src:29 | (1) For the purposes of section 3, a person meets the basic conditions who—
-- src:30 | (a) is at least 18 years old,
-- src:31 | (b) has not reached the qualifying age for state pension credit,
-- src:32 | (c) is in Great Britain,
-- src:33 | (d) is not receiving education, and
-- src:34 | (e) has accepted a claimant commitment.
@ref s 4(1) (src:29-34)
GIVEN `limb (a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (c)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (d)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `limb (e)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `regulations except the person from the basic condition they do not meet` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1) the person meets the basic conditions` MEANS
    CONSIDER `all five limbs`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): some limb is not known and no limb is known to fail
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN
        CONSIDER `regulations except the person from the basic condition they do not meet`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person meets the basic conditions who--" the five limbs; a supplied regulation says there is no exception
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
            IF `some limb is not known` THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): s 4(2) exceptions are not among the sources, so the "no" is provisional
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHERE
        `all five limbs` MEANS
            `s 3(1) both of these are true`
                (`s 3(1) both of these are true`
                    (`s 3(1) both of these are true` `limb (a)` `limb (b)`)
                    (`s 3(1) both of these are true` `limb (c)` `limb (d)`))
                `limb (e)`
        `is not known` m MEANS
            CONSIDER m
            WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (a helper, not a rule of the Act): the limb's value is known
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `some limb is not known` MEANS
            (`is not known` `limb (a)` OR `is not known` `limb (b)` OR `is not known` `limb (c)`)
            OR (`is not known` `limb (d)` OR `is not known` `limb (e)`)

§ `Section 5 -- Financial conditions`

-- (1)(a): capital, "or a prescribed part of it", "is not greater than a prescribed amount". The first input is the capital
-- that counts (the whole, or the part regulations prescribe); the limit is the prescribed amount.
-- src:48 | (a) the claimant's capital, or a prescribed part of it, is not greater than a prescribed amount, and
@ref s 5(1)(a) (src:48-48)
GIVEN `the claimant's capital, or the prescribed part of it` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed amount of capital` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(1)(a) the claimant's capital is not greater than the prescribed amount` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the prescribed amount of capital`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the financial conditions for a single claimant are that-- (a) ... is not greater than a prescribed amount" ("are that" defines them)
    WHEN JUST limit THEN JUST (`the claimant's capital, or the prescribed part of it` AT MOST limit)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the amount is prescribed; regulations are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- (1)(b): "the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum". If no minimum is prescribed the caller supplies 0.
-- src:49 | (b) the claimant's income is such that, if the claimant were entitled to universal credit, the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum.
@ref s 5(1)(b) (src:49-49)
GIVEN `the amount payable if the claimant were entitled to universal credit` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the prescribed minimum` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(1)(b) the amount payable would not be less than the prescribed minimum` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the amount payable if the claimant were entitled to universal credit`
    WHEN JUST payable THEN
        CONSIDER `the prescribed minimum`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the financial conditions ... are that ... the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum"
        WHEN JUST minimum THEN JUST (payable AT LEAST minimum)
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the minimum is prescribed
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the amount payable comes from s 8, whose inputs the regulations supply
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:47 | (1) For the purposes of section 3, the financial conditions for a single claimant are that—
-- src:48 | (a) the claimant's capital, or a prescribed part of it, is not greater than a prescribed amount, and
-- src:49 | (b) the claimant's income is such that, if the claimant were entitled to universal credit, the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum.
@ref s 5(1) (src:47-49)
GIVEN `capital condition (a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `income condition (b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(1) the financial conditions for a single claimant are met` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the financial conditions for a single claimant are that-- (a) ... and (b) ..."
    `s 3(1) both of these are true` `capital condition (a)` `income condition (b)`

-- src:51 | (a) their combined capital, or a prescribed part of it, is not greater than a prescribed amount, and
@ref s 5(2)(a) (src:51-51)
GIVEN `their combined capital, or the prescribed part of it` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed amount of capital` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(2)(a) their combined capital is not greater than the prescribed amount` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the prescribed amount of capital`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the financial conditions for joint claimants are that-- (a) their combined capital, or a prescribed part of it, is not greater than a prescribed amount"
    WHEN JUST limit THEN JUST (`their combined capital, or the prescribed part of it` AT MOST limit)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the amount is prescribed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:52 | (b) their combined income is such that, if they were entitled to universal credit, the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum.
@ref s 5(2)(b) (src:52-52)
GIVEN `the amount payable if they were entitled to universal credit` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the prescribed minimum` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(2)(b) the amount payable would not be less than the prescribed minimum` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the amount payable if they were entitled to universal credit`
    WHEN JUST payable THEN
        CONSIDER `the prescribed minimum`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "their combined income is such that ... the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum"
        WHEN JUST minimum THEN JUST (payable AT LEAST minimum)
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the minimum is prescribed
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the amount payable comes from s 8, whose inputs the regulations supply
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:50 | (2) For those purposes, the financial conditions for joint claimants are that—
-- src:51 | (a) their combined capital, or a prescribed part of it, is not greater than a prescribed amount, and
-- src:52 | (b) their combined income is such that, if they were entitled to universal credit, the amount payable would not be less than any prescribed minimum.
@ref s 5(2) (src:50-52)
GIVEN `capital condition (a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `income condition (b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(2) the financial conditions for joint claimants are met` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the financial conditions for joint claimants are that-- (a) ... and (b) ..."
    `s 3(1) both of these are true` `capital condition (a)` `income condition (b)`

§ `Section 6 -- Restrictions on entitlement`

-- "Entitlement to universal credit does not arise (a) in prescribed circumstances ...; (b) if the requirements in section 3
-- are met for a period shorter than a prescribed period; (c) for a prescribed period at the beginning of a period during
-- which those requirements are met." TRUE here means the entitlement does not arise; FALSE means that no limb restricts it.
-- s 6(3): regulations may provide for exceptions to (1)(b) or (c); where supplied as JUST TRUE they remove limbs (b) and (c).
-- src:59 | (3) Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1)(b) or (c).
@ref s 6(3) (src:59-59)
-- src:54 | (1) Entitlement to universal credit does not arise—
-- src:55 | (a) in prescribed circumstances (even though the requirements in section 3 are met);
-- src:56 | (b) if the requirements in section 3 are met for a period shorter than a prescribed period;
-- src:57 | (c) for a prescribed period at the beginning of a period during which those requirements are met.
@ref s 6(1) (src:54-57)
GIVEN `prescribed circumstances apply` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the days the requirements in section 3 have been met for` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed minimum period in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the person is within the prescribed period at the beginning` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)(b) or (c)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 6(1) entitlement to universal credit does not arise` MEANS
    `s 19(2) either of these is true` `limb (a)`
        (`s 19(2) either of these is true` `limb (b)` `limb (c)`)
    WHERE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the circumstances are prescribed; where supplied the answer is the regulations' own
        `limb (a)` MEANS `prescribed circumstances apply`
        `limb (b)` MEANS
            CONSIDER `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)(b) or (c)`
            -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): "Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1)(b) or (c)"
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
            OTHERWISE
                CONSIDER `the prescribed minimum period in days`
                WHEN JUST minimum THEN
                    IF `the days the requirements in section 3 have been met for` LESS THAN minimum
                    THEN JUST TRUE
                    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the requirements in section 3 are met for a period shorter than a prescribed period" (a failed "if": lawyer's reading (a))
                    ELSE JUST FALSE
                -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the period is prescribed
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        `limb (c)` MEANS
            CONSIDER `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)(b) or (c)`
            -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): "Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1)(b) or (c)"
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "a prescribed period at the beginning"; where supplied the answer is the regulations' own
            OTHERWISE `the person is within the prescribed period at the beginning`

-- s 6(2): "A period prescribed under subsection (1)(b) or (c) may not exceed seven days."
-- src:58 | (2) A period prescribed under subsection (1)(b) or (c) may not exceed seven days.
@ref s 6(2) (src:58-58)
GIVEN `the period regulations prescribe under subsection (1)(b) or (c), in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) the prescribed period is within the seven-day limit` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period regulations prescribe under subsection (1)(b) or (c), in days`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may not exceed seven days"
    WHEN JUST days THEN JUST (days AT MOST 7)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the period is prescribed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `Section 7 -- Basis of awards`

-- (1) "payable in respect of each complete assessment period within a period of entitlement": complete means every day of the
-- assessment period lies inside the period of entitlement (both ends count, wra-core).
-- src:62 | (1) Universal credit is payable in respect of each complete assessment period within a period of entitlement.
@ref s 7(1) (src:62-62)
GIVEN `the assessment period` IS A Period
      `the period of entitlement` IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 7(1) universal credit is payable in respect of the assessment period` MEANS
    IF `the assessment period`'s `first day` AT LEAST `the period of entitlement`'s `first day`
       AND `the assessment period`'s `last day` AT MOST `the period of entitlement`'s `last day`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): only a "complete assessment period within a period of entitlement" is covered; s 7(3)(b) lets regulations make a shorter period payable
    ELSE NOTHING

-- (2) "an 'assessment period' is a period of a prescribed duration"; the duration is an input in days.
-- src:63 | (2) In this Part an “assessment period” is a period of a prescribed duration.
@ref s 7(2) (src:63-63)
GIVEN period IS A Period
      `the duration regulations prescribe, in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 7(2) the period is an assessment period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the duration regulations prescribe, in days`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an 'assessment period' is a period of a prescribed duration"
    WHEN JUST days THEN JUST ((Day (period's `last day`)) MINUS (Day (period's `first day`)) PLUS 1 EQUALS days)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the duration is prescribed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- (4) "period of entitlement" means a period during which entitlement to universal credit subsists.
-- src:68 | (4) In subsection (1) “period of entitlement” means a period during which entitlement to universal credit subsists.
@ref s 7(4) (src:68-68)
GIVEN `entitlement to universal credit subsists on every day of the period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 7(4) the period is a period of entitlement` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "'period of entitlement' means a period during which entitlement to universal credit subsists"
    `entitlement to universal credit subsists on every day of the period`

§ `Section 8 -- Calculation of awards`

-- (4) the income the deductions refer to: a single claimant's own income, or the combined income of joint claimants.
-- src:81 | (4) In subsection (3)(a) and (b) the references to income are—
-- src:82 | (a) in the case of a single claimant, to income of the claimant, and
-- src:83 | (b) in the case of joint claimants, to combined income of the claimants.
@ref s 8(4) (src:81-83)
GIVEN claimants IS A Claimants
      `the income of the single claimant, or of the first of joint claimants` IS A NUMBER
      `the income of the second of joint claimants` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 8(4) the income to which the deductions refer` MEANS
    CONSIDER claimants
    WHEN `a single claimant` person THEN `the income of the single claimant, or of the first of joint claimants`
    WHEN `joint claimants` two THEN
        `the income of the single claimant, or of the first of joint claimants` PLUS `the income of the second of joint claimants`

-- (2) the maximum amount is the total of the amounts included under ss 9 to 12; any amount not included is 0, supplied by the caller.
-- src:73 | (2) The maximum amount is the total of—
-- src:74 | (a) any amount included under section 9 (standard allowance),
-- src:75 | (b) any amount included under section 10 (responsibility for children and young persons),
-- src:76 | (c) any amount included under section 11 (housing costs), and
-- src:77 | (d) any amount included under section 12 (other particular needs or circumstances).
@ref s 8(2) (src:73-77)
GIVEN `the sum` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `next amount` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 8(2) the sum of two possibly unknown amounts` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the sum`
    WHEN JUST a THEN
        CONSIDER `next amount`
        WHEN JUST b THEN JUST (a PLUS b)
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the amounts under ss 9 to 12 are specified by regulations
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

@ref s 8(2) (src:73-77)
GIVEN `the amount included under section 9 (standard allowance)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount included under section 10 (responsibility for children and young persons)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount included under section 11 (housing costs)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount included under section 12 (other particular needs or circumstances)` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 8(2) the maximum amount` MEANS
    `s 8(2) the sum of two possibly unknown amounts`
        (`s 8(2) the sum of two possibly unknown amounts`
            (`s 8(2) the sum of two possibly unknown amounts` `the amount included under section 9 (standard allowance)` `the amount included under section 10 (responsibility for children and young persons)`)
            `the amount included under section 11 (housing costs)`)
        `the amount included under section 12 (other particular needs or circumstances)`

-- (3)(a)-(b): the amounts to be deducted are calculated "in the prescribed manner (which may include multiplying some or all
-- income by a prescribed percentage)". The one manner the Act names is encoded: the income that is multiplied, times the
-- percentage. Any other prescribed manner is for the caller to supply as the amount itself.
-- src:78 | (3) The amounts to be deducted are—
-- src:79 | (a) an amount in respect of earned income calculated in the prescribed manner (which may include multiplying some or all earned income by a prescribed percentage), and
@ref s 8(3)(a) (src:78-79)
GIVEN `the earned income that is multiplied by the prescribed percentage` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed percentage, as a number of hundredths` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 8(3)(a) the amount deducted in respect of earned income` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the prescribed percentage, as a number of hundredths`
    WHEN JUST percentage THEN JUST (`the earned income that is multiplied by the prescribed percentage` TIMES percentage DIVIDED BY 100)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the percentage and the manner are prescribed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:80 | (b) an amount in respect of unearned income calculated in the prescribed manner (which may include multiplying some or all unearned income by a prescribed percentage).
@ref s 8(3)(b) (src:80-80)
GIVEN `the unearned income that is multiplied by the prescribed percentage` IS A NUMBER
      `the prescribed percentage, as a number of hundredths` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 8(3)(b) the amount deducted in respect of unearned income` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the prescribed percentage, as a number of hundredths`
    WHEN JUST percentage THEN JUST (`the unearned income that is multiplied by the prescribed percentage` TIMES percentage DIVIDED BY 100)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the percentage and the manner are prescribed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- (1) "the amount of an award ... is to be the balance of (a) the maximum amount, less (b) the amounts to be deducted".
-- The Act does not say what happens when the deductions exceed the maximum amount; the balance is reported as it is
-- (see the fork register in NOTES.md). Seam 2 of PLAN.md takes this function's result.
-- src:70 | (1) The amount of an award of universal credit is to be the balance of—
-- src:71 | (a) the maximum amount (see subsection (2)), less
-- src:72 | (b) the amounts to be deducted (see subsection (3)).
@ref s 8(1) (src:70-72)
GIVEN `the maximum amount` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount deducted in respect of earned income` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the amount deducted in respect of unearned income` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 8(1) the amount of the award of universal credit for the period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the maximum amount`
    WHEN JUST maximum THEN
        CONSIDER `s 8(2) the sum of two possibly unknown amounts` `the amount deducted in respect of earned income` `the amount deducted in respect of unearned income`
        WHEN JUST deductions THEN JUST (maximum MINUS deductions)
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the deductions are calculated in the prescribed manner
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the maximum amount is the total of amounts regulations specify
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `Section 9 -- Standard allowance`

-- (1) "The calculation of an award of universal credit is to include an amount by way of an allowance for (a) a single
-- claimant, or (b) joint claimants." (3) "Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1)": where supplied as JUST
-- TRUE the allowance is not included; the Act's own "is to include" otherwise stands. (2) the amount is specified by regulations.
-- src:90 | (3) Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1).
@ref s 9(3) (src:90-90)
-- src:86 | (1) The calculation of an award of universal credit is to include an amount by way of an allowance for—
-- src:87 | (a) a single claimant, or
-- src:88 | (b) joint claimants.
@ref s 9(1) (src:86-88)
GIVEN claimants IS A Claimants
      `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1) for these claimants` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 9(1) the calculation of the award includes the standard allowance` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1) for these claimants`
    -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): "Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1)"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER claimants
        WHEN `a single claimant` person THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `joint claimants` two THEN JUST TRUE

§ `Section 10 -- Responsibility for children and young persons`

-- (5) "qualifying young person" means a person of a prescribed description.
-- src:97 | (5) In this Part, “qualifying young person” means a person of a prescribed description.
@ref s 10(5) (src:97-97)
GIVEN `the person is of a description regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 10(5) the person is a qualifying young person` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): "'qualifying young person' means a person of a prescribed description"
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the description is prescribed; regulations are not among the sources
    `the person is of a description regulations prescribe`

-- (1) "an amount for each child or qualifying young person for whom a claimant is responsible"; whether a claimant is
-- responsible for a person is for regulations (Schedule 1 paragraph 5(1)), and (4) "Regulations may provide for exceptions
-- to subsection (1)". A person who is neither a child nor a qualifying young person, or for whom no claimant is
-- responsible, is outside the grant.
-- src:96 | (4) Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1) ....
@ref s 10(4) (src:96-96)
-- src:92 | (1) The calculation of an award of universal credit is to include an amount for each child or qualifying young person for whom a claimant is responsible.
@ref s 10(1) (src:92-92)
GIVEN person IS A Person
      `a claimant is responsible for the person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person is a qualifying young person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 10(1) the calculation of the award includes an amount for the person` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the grant applies`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)`
        -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): "Regulations may provide for exceptions to subsection (1)"
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "each child or qualifying young person for whom a claimant is responsible"; a person outside that description is not covered
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): responsibility and the description of a qualifying young person are for regulations
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHERE
        `the grant applies` MEANS
            `s 3(1) both of these are true`
                `a claimant is responsible for the person`
                (`s 19(2) either of these is true` (JUST (`s 40 the person is a child` person)) `the person is a qualifying young person`)

§ `Section 11 -- Housing costs`

-- (2) "(a) the accommodation must be in Great Britain; (b) the accommodation must be residential accommodation; (c) it is
-- immaterial whether the accommodation consists of the whole or part of a building ...", which adds no condition.
-- src:100 | (2) For the purposes of subsection (1)—
-- src:101 | (a) the accommodation must be in Great Britain;
-- src:102 | (b) the accommodation must be residential accommodation;
-- src:103 | (c) it is immaterial whether the accommodation consists of the whole or part of a building and whether or not it comprises separate and self-contained premises.
@ref s 11(2) (src:100-103)
GIVEN `where the accommodation is` IS A Place
      `the accommodation is residential accommodation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 11(2) the accommodation is accommodation that housing costs may be included for` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the accommodation must be in Great Britain" and "must be residential accommodation"
    `s 4(1)(c) the place is in Great Britain` `where the accommodation is`
    AND `the accommodation is residential accommodation`

-- (1) "an amount in respect of any liability of a claimant to make payments in respect of the accommodation they occupy as
-- their home". Regulations say when a claimant is treated as liable (s 11(3)(b)) or as occupying (s 11(3)(c)), and may
-- provide exceptions (s 11(5)(a)); each is an input.
-- src:106 | (b) circumstances in which a claimant is to be treated as liable or not liable to make such payments;
-- src:107 | (c) circumstances in which a claimant is to be treated as occupying or not occupying accommodation as their home (and, in particular, for temporary absences to be disregarded);
@ref s 11(3)(b)-(c) (src:106-107)
-- src:110 | (5) Regulations may—
-- src:111 | (a) provide for exceptions to subsection (1);
@ref s 11(5)(a) (src:110-111)
-- src:99 | (1) The calculation of an award of universal credit is to include an amount in respect of any liability of a claimant to make payments in respect of the accommodation they occupy as their home.
@ref s 11(1) (src:99-99)
GIVEN `the claimant is liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the claimant occupies the accommodation as their home` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the accommodation meets section 11(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(1) the calculation of the award includes an amount for housing costs` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the grant applies`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER `regulations provide an exception to subsection (1)`
        -- FALSE-kind (a supplied regulation says no): "Regulations may ... provide for exceptions to subsection (1)"
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any liability of a claimant to make payments in respect of the accommodation they occupy as their home"; a case outside that description is not covered
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): liability and occupation are for regulations to treat (s 11(3)(b), (c))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHERE
        `the grant applies` MEANS
            `s 3(1) both of these are true`
                (`s 3(1) both of these are true` `the claimant is liable to make payments in respect of the accommodation` `the claimant occupies the accommodation as their home`)
                (JUST `the accommodation meets section 11(2)`)

§ `Section 12 -- Other particular needs or circumstances`

-- (1) "amounts in respect of such particular needs or circumstances of a claimant as may be prescribed".
-- src:116 | (1) The calculation of an award of universal credit is to include amounts in respect of such particular needs or circumstances of a claimant as may be prescribed.
@ref s 12(1) (src:116-116)
GIVEN `regulations prescribe the need or circumstance` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(1) the calculation of the award includes an amount for the need or circumstance` MEANS
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "such particular needs or circumstances of a claimant as may be prescribed"; where supplied the answer is the regulations' own
    `regulations prescribe the need or circumstance`

-- (2) the prescribed needs or circumstances "may include" (b) and (c); the list is not closed, and (a) is blank in this text.
DECLARE `s 12(2) need or circumstance` IS ONE OF
    `s 12(2)(b) limited capability for work and work-related activity`
    `s 12(2)(c) regular and substantial caring responsibilities for a severely disabled person`
    `s 12(2) some other need or circumstance`

-- src:117 | (2) The needs or circumstances prescribed under subsection (1) may include—
-- src:118 | (a) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:119 | (b) the fact that a claimant has limited capability for work and work-related activity;
-- src:120 | (c) the fact that a claimant has regular and substantial caring responsibilities for a severely disabled person.
@ref s 12(2) (src:117-120)
GIVEN need IS A `s 12(2) need or circumstance`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(2) the needs or circumstances prescribed under subsection (1) may include` MEANS
    CONSIDER need
    WHEN `s 12(2)(b) limited capability for work and work-related activity` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 12(2)(c) regular and substantial caring responsibilities for a severely disabled person` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "may include" does not close the list
    OTHERWISE NOTHING
